 its L1 proofs use Keccak-256-based commitments instead of BN254 pairings. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nReleases · PolyhedraZK/Expander · GitHub (https://github.com/PolyhedraZK/Expander/releases)\nciteturn6search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago;   * Sumcheck opt by @niconiconi in #81 ...   * stats code & release binary by @zhiyong1997 in #135\n  * Squash merge GF2_dev into main, accepting all GF2_dev changes by @niconiconi in #79\n  * Sumcheck opt by @niconiconi in #81\n  * handle layer with zero var. by @zhiyong1997 in #84\n  * Implement more field operations for ECC by @mcfx in #86\n  * avx256 by @chonpsk in #88\n  * Proof size by @zhiyong1997 in #87\n  * fix_pack by @niconiconi in #89\n  * Verification time by @zhiyong1997 in #90\n  * update CI by @zhenfeizhang in #102\n  * remove avx512 instructions from avx256 by @mcfx in #94\n  * [FIX] m31 exp by @enpsi20481 in #96\n  * fix ci by @zhenfeizhang in #104\n  * Mpi by @zhiyong1997 in #93\n  * use avx256 by default by @zhenfeizhang in #109\n  * Update readme.md by @siq1 in #110\n  * refactoring field impl by @zhenfeizhang in #95\n  * transcript refactor by @zhenfeizhang in #103\n  * Public input rebase by @zhiyong1997 in #108\n  * refactor sumcheck by @zhenfeizhang in #112\n  * update main to v0.0.4 by @zhenfeizhang in #126\n  * fix power gate by @zhenfeizhang in #128\n  * Recursive Verifier by @zhiyong1997 in #127\n  * Lagrange Form Bi kzg update by @zhiyong1997 in #130\n  * [impl] adding sumcheck-cuda by @slzk in #131\n  * stats code & release binary by @zhiyong1997 in #135\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEin MVP kostet ein Start-up in Deutschland typischerweise rund 24.000 €. Bei mir kostet dasselbe 10.500 €. Ich habe gerade Sumcheck in den App Store gebracht. Konzept, Design, Code, Einreichung… | Simon Giesen (https://de.linkedin.com/posts/simon-giesen-99b0441a4_ein-mvp-kostet-ein-start-up-in-deutschland-activity-7510924307968040960--hzZ)\nciteturn6search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 weeks ago; Ich habe gerade Sumcheck in den App Store gebracht. Konzept, Design, Code, Einreichung, Release — alles selbst. ... Gilt nur für Aufträge, die 2026 starten.\n\n# Beitrag von Simon Giesen\n\nSimon Giesen\n\n1 Std.\n\nEin MVP kostet ein Start-up in Deutschland typischerweise rund 24.000 €. Bei mir kostet dasselbe 10.500 €. Ich habe gerade Sumcheck in den App Store gebracht. Konzept, Design, Code, Einreichung, Release — alles selbst. Und dabei ist mir nochmal klar geworden, wie viel von dem, was üblicherweise abgerechnet wird, gar nicht in der App landet, sondern in Abstimmung zwischen Leuten, die alle nicht bauen. Deshalb mache ich bis Jahresende etwas, das sich für mich nicht rechnen, sondern Referenzen bringen soll: 350 € netto am Tag statt der marktüblichen 800 €. Drin ist alles, was du brauchst, um live zu gehen: → Konzept und Scoping — zugeschnitten auf das, was deine Idee wirklich beweisen muss → UX- und UI-Design → Entwicklung, auf der dein Team später weiterbauen kann → Tests auf echten Geräten, bevor deine ersten Nutzer die Fehler finden → App-Store-Release inklusive Einreichung, Screenshots und Review → Übergabe und Doku, damit ihr jederzeit ohne mich weitermachen könnt Kein Aufschlag fürs Design. Keine Extraposition fürs Projektmanagement. Kein Nachtrag für den Release. Bei 30 Entwicklertagen bleiben 13.500 € in deiner Kasse. Das sind je nach Runde ein paar Monate zusätzlicher Runway — oder genau das Marketingbudget, das sonst gefehlt hätte. Ein Ansprechpartner. Wer plant, baut auch, und ist direkt erreichbar. Gilt nur für Aufträge, die 2026 starten. Wenn du dein MVP gerade vor dir herschiebst, weil das letzte Angebot zu teuer war: schreib mir. 2 Slots bei mir sind noch übriggeblieben.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAztecProtocol/aztec-packages v6.0.0 on GitHub (https://newreleases.io/project/github/AztecProtocol/aztec-packages/release/v6.0.0)\nciteturn6search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: last week; Crawled: 6 days ago;   * One multilinear-batching sumcheck per kernel (`635a9cf`). ...   * A rewritten Pippenger MSM (`3c7386e`) and Booth-sliced element MSMs (#23691).\n\n## Proving system (Barretenberg)\n\nChonk (client IVC)\n\n  * Per-kind circuit flavors. Apps and kernels now have their own flavors, `MegaApp` and `MegaKernel` (`ff378cb`), with a `CircuitKind` carried from the PXE through bb.js and bbapi. Protocol VKs are computed per kind at the source, replacing `--use_zk_flavor`.\n  * One multilinear-batching sumcheck per kernel (`635a9cf`).\n  * Custom arithmetic gates for kernels (`b7433c0`), and room for 5 apps per kernel instead of 3 (`910504d`).\n  * ROM tables via logup (`d3e42f9`).\n  * Cheaper Poseidon2: two fewer rows per permutation (`f14df1d`) and batched sponge absorbs (`f7b101b`).\n\nPCS, IPA and the Grumpkin side\n\n  * TripleIPA for the ECCVM (`bcb979d`), with fused IPA folds and a short-scalar IPA SRS fold.\n  * A sparse ZK masking polynomial in Shplemini (`1a42ab0`), and Libra masking with `L = 4` on Grumpkin (`19052d3`).\n  * ECCVM and Translator sumcheck optimizations (#23615), plus general sumcheck round and relation evaluation speed-ups.\n  * The Grumpkin SRS is anchored to pinned chunk hashes on load (#23587).\n\nPerformance\n\n  * A rewritten Pippenger MSM (`3c7386e`) and Booth-sliced element MSMs (#23691).\n  * WASM SIMD throughout the prover: a 5-wide `VectorField` primitive, Montgomery multiplication, row-parallel sumcheck univariates, and SIMD Gemini folds and `partially_evaluate`.\n  * Faster circuit construction (`398a5e6`): arithmetic, tiled gate storage and containers.\n\nAVM\n\n  * A specialized MegaAVM flavor (`a32b5b8`), per-column public-input sizes in the recursive verifier, and granular destination selectors for precomputed subtraces.\n  * The TypeScript AVM simulator is gone. Simulation runs in the native `bb-avm-sim` service over generated IPC bindings (`96082e3`), alongside a `wsdb` world-state service and the new `ipc-runtime`.\n  * Fixes from the Honk audit (`2c6023a`).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAbdelStark/tessera Good First Issues | Good First Issue (https://www.goodfirstissue.org/AbdelStark/tessera)\nciteturn6search5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 days ago; AbdelStark/tessera#487 · Opened Oct 24, 2026 ... area:prover area:sumcheck effort:m priority:p2 spec:rfc-0009 spec:rfc-0010 type:perf\narea:pcs area:zk spec:rfc-0012 type:security\n\nAbdelStark/tessera#487 · Opened Oct 24, 2026\n\nMaintainers usually reply within 1 day\n\n  * spec: tessera-protocol/2 must not reveal the leaf stage's per-layer split values\n\narea:zk spec:rfc-0013 type:security\n\nAbdelStark/tessera#486 · Opened Oct 24, 2026\n\nMaintainers usually reply within 1 day\n\n  * spec: the weight linking proof reveals W̃_T(ρ_T) in tessera-protocol/2\n\narea:pcs area:zk spec:rfc-0012 type:security\n\nAbdelStark/tessera#485 · Opened Oct 24, 2026\n\nMaintainers usually reply within 1 day\n\n  * spec: prove a request longer than one proof holds: chunk linking and long-context proof size\n\npriority:p1 type:docs\n\nAbdelStark/tessera#470 · Opened Oct 24, 2026\n\nMaintainers usually reply within 1 day\n\n  * prover: score instances without the score tensor, and shared instances (RFC-0056)\n\narea:prover area:sumcheck effort:m priority:p2 spec:rfc-0009 spec:rfc-0010 type:perf\n\nDifficulty 5/5 Over a week Newbie friendliness 35/100\n\nAbdelStark/tessera#461 · Opened Oct 23, 2026\n\nMaintainers usually reply within 1 day\n\n  * sumcheck: windowed rounds for reduction stages larger than memory (RFC-0055)\n\narea:prover area:sumcheck effort:l priority:p2 spec:rfc-0010 spec:rfc-0018 type:perf\n\nDifficulty 5/5 Over a week Newbie friendliness 25/100\n\nAbdelStark/tessera#459 · Opened Oct 23, 2026\n\nMaintainers usually reply within 1 day\n\n  * spec: reconcile mask one-use with full p